WEBVTT

00:01.280 --> 00:05.840
Every month, someone has to build a schedule for all your employees.

00:06.160 --> 00:08.320
Usually, it's done in a spreadsheet,

00:08.640 --> 00:12.000
and usually, the same mistakes come back again and again.

00:12.480 --> 00:15.760
A forgotten rest day, not enough staff on Saturday,

00:16.280 --> 00:17.900
hours that are wrong.

00:18.100 --> 00:20.040
This is ShiftSmith,

00:20.280 --> 00:24.840
a scheduling tool I built for a hotel with 28 employees.

00:25.160 --> 00:28.920
Each square is one employee on one day.

00:29.240 --> 00:33.760
Move your mouse onto a square, and you can see

00:33.770 --> 00:36.400
the shift and the hours of a specific person.

00:37.600 --> 00:42.240
Now, the most important part: ShiftSmith checks the rules

00:42.250 --> 00:45.040
while you plan. Rest days,

00:45.280 --> 00:47.680
minimum staff, contract hours.

00:48.130 --> 00:51.970
When something breaks a rule, the square turns red.

00:52.290 --> 00:56.850
For example, here, Tomasz Nowak is not eligible

00:56.850 --> 00:59.170
for the D shift at the reception,

00:59.410 --> 01:01.810
which is from midnight to 7:30.

01:03.090 --> 01:05.690
You don't need to find the fix yourself.

01:06.010 --> 01:10.890
Just click "Solve", and ShiftSmith suggests changes

01:10.890 --> 01:13.770
that fix the problem without creating a new one.

01:14.250 --> 01:18.490
For example, turning D into B shifts for Tomasz.

01:18.810 --> 01:23.490
I can apply it either to a draft,

01:23.490 --> 01:26.450
or directly to the published schedule.

01:26.770 --> 01:30.290
This will solve the problem without creating new ones.

01:30.850 --> 01:33.730
One click, and the schedule is correct again.

01:34.050 --> 01:37.060
No more mistakes found on Monday morning.
